#
# Email when a path wansentry manages goes down or comes back.
#
# `connected` / `disconnected` are mwan3track's verdicts (probes passing or
# failing over the up/down thresholds), not netifd link events, so a flapping
# cable does not produce a mail per flap: mwan3's hysteresis has already
# filtered it. Runs the sender in the background so a slow SMTP server can
# never hold up the hotplug chain that the failover itself runs on.

case "$ACTION" in connected|disconnected) ;; *) exit 0 ;; esac

# mwan3 sends "disconnected" for every online path when it is merely STOPPED
# (a restart, a reload, a Save & Apply that changes its config), and marks those
# events MWAN3_SHUTDOWN=1 (mwan3_interface_hotplug_shutdown in mwan3.sh). Nothing
# failed; mailing "DOWN" for each was a false alarm per path. Measured on the
# GL-MT6000 2026-10-10: one restart, four "is DOWN" emails.
[ "$MWAN3_SHUTDOWN" = 1 ] && exit 0
[ -n "$INTERFACE" ] || exit 0
[ "$(uci -q get wansentry.notify.enabled)" = 1 ] || exit 0
[ "$(uci -q get "mwan3.$INTERFACE.wansentry")" = 1 ] || exit 0

# `1000>&-` closes procd's service lock for the background sender. When mwan3's
# own init script runs the hotplug chain it holds /var/lock/procd_mwan3.lock on
# fd 1000, and a child that inherits the fd holds the lock for as long as it
# lives. On the GL-MT6000 2026-10-10 three senders hung in msmtp (mwan3 had
# flushed conntrack under their SMTP sessions), kept the lock for ten minutes,
# every mwan3 tracker blocked on it, and the killswitch held the tunnel half
# dark. A notification must never be able to stop the failover it reports.
# busybox ash handles fds above 9 (checked on the EA8500 2026-10-10); dash does
# not, but hotplug scripts run under /bin/sh, which on OpenWrt is ash.
# shellcheck disable=SC3023
/usr/libexec/wansentry/notify event "$INTERFACE" "$ACTION" </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 1000>&- &
exit 0
